{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant, who always provide explanation. Think like you are answering to a five year old."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Write a title for this article:\n\nWhen the United Nations Security Council passed its latest sanctions resolution on Iran, on June 9, President Barack Obama hailed it as \"the toughest sanctions ever faced by the Iranian government.\" This resolution, he said, \"sends an unmistakable message about the international community's commitment to stopping the spread of nuclear weapons.\"\n\nNot yet, it doesn't. On such matters as money, weapons and terror, the so-called international community, whatever its U.N. trappings, is a snake pit in which the 192 member states are most reliably committed to first serving themselves. For some, that has already entailed a certain tolerance for the spread of nuclear weapons, if it means they might either hope to get some of their own, or profit from the nuclear quests of others. The U.N. is a place where one man's sanctions are another man's ticket to sanctions-busting profits--no matter how lofty the stack of resolutions.\n\nIf Obama now wants to bet on sanctions as the tool of choice for stopping Iran's nuclear program, it is the U.S., not the U.N., that will have to provide the real leadership and heavy lifting--for the simple reason that the U.N. won't. That's why Obama should lose no time in signing the new Iran sanctions legislation that Congress has finally sent to his desk, approved this week with votes of 99-0 in the Senate, and 408-8 in the House. This bill aims to close loopholes that have allowed Iran to press on toward the bomb, bragging up increased uranium enrichment despite a patchwork of longstanding U.S. sanctions, plus three previous U.N. sanctions resolutions in 2006, 2007 and 2008.\n\nThe weakness of U.N. sanctions is that the U.N. itself lacks both the integrity and the functional policing mechanisms to back them up. Whatever the \"binding\" words issued by the Security Council, the U.N. relies on member states to formulate their own rules for domestic compliance. They are then supposed to police and report back on themselves.\n\nAt best, the U.N. appoints a multilateral panel of experts to monitor this process. Beyond naming and shaming (which they are usually pressured by the U.N. to avoid) they have no power to penalize, or even to compel uncooperative counties to let them in. In the case of North Korea sanctions, for instance, the U.N. panel of experts has yet to be allowed to poke around inside North Korea's mainstay and chief trading partner, China. And though all U.N. members are supposed to submit reports on what they are doing to comply with sanctions, in the case of Iran the U.N. website shows that more than one-third of the U.N.'s member states have yet to report in at all. Among the absentees are such major Iranian partners as Syria and Lebanon. Turkey, which voted against the latest sanctions, appears to have last clocked in on its own compliance with prior resolutions back in 2007.\n\nFor a taste of how wrong things can go when the U.N. is trusted to take the lead, recall the previous push via sanctions to defang a predatory oil-rich Middle East regime. That would be the case of Saddam Hussein's Iraq, where the U.N. in theory had far tighter controls than anything currently on the table for Iran. All Saddam Hussein's oil sales, and all goods bought with the proceeds, were supposed to be handled and overseen via the U.N. That plan ran from 1996-2003, and was known as Oil-for-Food.\n\nWhat ensued was a bonanza of sanctions busting, in which many member states did nothing to police violations on their own turf, and some governments were themselves complicit. It was only the U.S.-led overthrow of Saddam in 2003 that finally brought to light a hoard of Baghdad records so incriminating that the U.N. was finally forced into mandating a special inquiry from 2004-05, led at a cost of more than $35 million by Paul Volcker. What lessons did sanctions busters learn from that experience?\n\nIn the U.S., thanks more to congressional and federal investigators than to the U.N. inquiry, a parade of private players faced prosecutions, resulting in an assortment of fines, plea bargains, convictions and jail time. But among some of the worst violators, in places such as Russia, China, Syria, Egypt, Jordan, Turkey and Lebanon, there were no signs of any penalties at all. Among the buyers of oil, the U.N. committee identified 139 companies worldwide whose purchases were associated with a \"surcharge,\" or sanctions-busting kickback, to the government of Iraq. The committee sent letters to 127 of these companies, asking them what they had to say about this. Only 26 even bothered to respond.\n\nAmong sellers of \"humanitarian\" goods to Iraq, the scene was even more outrageous. Based on its findings, the U.N. inquiry sent letters, alleging illicit activity, to 2,253 companies worldwide. Only 293 replied.\n\nTitle:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The Challenge of Stopping Iran's Nuclear Program: Can Sanctions Work Without Stronger Leadership?"}]}
