{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. User will you give you a task. Your goal is to complete the task as faithfully as you can. While performing the task think step-by-step and justify your steps."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Here is a goal: coffee flavored cookies\n\nWhich way makes more sense to accomplish this goal?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "To accomplish the goal of making coffee flavored cookies, the most sensible approach would be to create a cookie recipe that incorporates coffee as one of its key flavoring agents. Here's a step-by-step plan for making delicious coffee-flavored cookies:\n\nStep 1: Gather ingredients and tools\nTo make coffee flavored cookies, we will need the following ingredients:\n- 1 cup (2 sticks) unsalted butter, softened\n- 1 cup granulated sugar\n- 1 large egg\n- 1 tsp vanilla extract\n- 2 1/2 cups all-purpose flour\n- 1/2 tsp baking powder\n- 1/2 tsp salt\n- 2-3 tbsp instant coffee granules or espresso powder (adjust to desired coffee flavor intensity)\n- Optional: 1 cup chocolate chips or chopped nuts (walnuts, pecans, etc.) for added texture and flavor\n\nWe will also need these kitchen tools:\n- Electric mixer (hand mixer or stand mixer)\n- Spatula\n- Mixing bowls\n- Measuring spoons and cups\n- Cookie sheets\n- Parchment paper or silicone baking mats\n- Wire cooling rack\n\nStep 2: Preheat the oven and prepare the cookie sheets\nPreheat the oven to 350°F (180°C). Line the cookie sheets with parchment paper or silicone baking mats to prevent sticking.\n\nStep 3: Mix the dry ingredients\nIn a medium-sized bowl, combine the flour, baking powder, salt, and instant coffee granules or espresso powder. Mix well to evenly distribute the coffee granules throughout the dry ingredients.\n\nStep 4: Cream the butter and sugar\nUsing the electric mixer, cream together the softened butter and granulated sugar in a large mixing bowl until light and fluffy. This will help incorporate air into the dough, which will result in a more tender cookie.\n\nStep 5: Add the egg and vanilla\nAdd the egg and vanilla extract to the butter-sugar mixture and beat until well combined.\n\nStep 6: Add the dry ingredients\nGradually incorporate the dry ingredient mixture into the wet ingredients, mixing until just combined. Be careful not to overmix, as this could result in tough cookies.\n\nStep 7: Fold in optional mix-ins\nIf desired, fold in chocolate chips or chopped nuts to add extra flavor and texture to the cookies.\n\nStep 8: Scoop and shape the cookies\nUsing a cookie scoop or spoon, portion out the dough into small balls and place them onto the prepared cookie sheets, spacing them evenly and allowing enough room for the cookies to spread during baking.\n\nStep 9: Bake the cookies\nBake the cookies in the preheated oven for 10-12 minutes, or until they are lightly golden and set around the edges. The centers may appear slightly undercooked, but they will continue to firm up as the cookies cool.\n\nStep 10: Cool and store the cookies\nOnce the cookies are done baking, let them cool on the cookie sheets for about 5 minutes before transferring them to a wire cooling rack to cool completely. Store the cooled cookies in an airtight container.\n\nBy following these steps, we will have successfully created delicious coffee-flavored cookies that satisfy the given goal."}]}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. You will be given a task. You must generate a detailed and long answer."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Summarize:\n\nRynard Landman's try helped the Welsh side into an early 10-0 lead, but two superbly-taken tries from Nick Grigg gave Warriors the initiative.\nRory Hughes, Ratu Tagive, Lee Jones, Mark Bennett and Peter Horne all touched down before Tyler Morgan scored a late try for the visitors.\nThe win moves Glasgow within six points of fifth-placed Ulster.\nThe Dragons had just shipped 54 points to Leinster so their early lead must have come as a shock to Glasgow, Angus O'Brien, their fly-half, putting them ahead with the boot, and then putting in a lovely grubber kick to the corner that put Gregor Townsend's team on the back foot.\nPinned in their own 22, Glasgow couldn't escape. When the Dragons came infield Landman stretched for the line, referee Andrew Brace with the help of his TMO giving the try even though it looked like Landman had got the ball down just short. O'Brien banged over the conversion to make it an unlikely 10-0 for the visitors who came into this on the back of 12 defeats in their 16 Pro12 games.\nGlasgow desperately needed a four-try bonus point win to keep their already slim semi-final hopes alive and they required a whole lot more ball to get it. Soon enough, they got on top. Tim Swinson and Brian Alainu'uese carried and carried and slowly ground the Dragons down.\nNick Grigg, terrific throughout, got his first just before the half hour, spinning through weak Dragons defence to score. Then he added another, a lovely solo run where he ripped through an increasingly porous Dragons. Horne converted both and Glasgow were ahead at 14-10.\nThey suffered the loss through injury of Richie Vernon just before half-time - poor Vernon has been cursed these past years - but they kicked on regardless. Just after the restart, Hughes blasted though more soft defending to get Glasgow's 