wners in downtown Chicago to enter into an agreement with the district cooling system known as Thermal Chicago, contributing to the national effort to reduce ozone-damaging CFCs.\n\n6. Clean Air Counts initiative: In 2006, the Mart joined the Clean Air Counts initiative, aimed at reducing smog-forming pollutants and energy consumption. Strategies included using low VOC cleaning products, paints, building materials, energy-efficient lighting, and promoting alternative workplace transportation options.\n\n7. Waste and water conservation: The Mart's recycling program saved over 13,000 trees and recycled nearly 11 million pounds of waste. Additionally, water conservation efforts saved 5.5 million US gallons of water.\n\n8. Greener transportation: More than eight percent of the estimated 10,000 people working at the Mart walk, bike, or take public transportation. The Mart also expanded bicycle storage capacity to more than 200 to encourage greener transportation methods.\n\n9. Converting stationery to 100% post-consumer recycled product: In 2009, the Mart converted its stationery to a fully recycled product.\n\n10. LEED-certified showrooms: The Mart has nine LEED-certified showrooms, with five others on their way to achieving LEED certification."}]}
