ansition creates even more pressure within the confined space of the can. The added pressure from the boiling soup further boosts the internal pressure level. \n\nThe sum of these effects can easily result in a pressure that exceeds the structural capacity of the can to contain it. Eventually, this intense internal pressure might lead to the can exploding. If unvented, as in the case with Team A, the pressure has nowhere to go but to force its way out by rupturing the walls of the can. \n\nOn the other hand, Team B vented their can before heating it. This means they created an outlet for the pressure to escape, moderating the level of pressure that could build up inside the can. Consequently, the pressure never reaches the point where it would be likely to cause an explosion, since the kinetic energy of the heated air and steam generated from boiling soup can dissipate through the vent hole. Therefore, it's safe to conclude that Team A's unvented can of soup was more likely to explode when heated over a campfire compared to Team B's vented can."}]}
