{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. User will you give you a task. Your goal is to complete the task as faithfully as you can. While performing the task think step-by-step and justify your steps."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Here is a news article: They are everywhere. In front of the Louvre Museum. Under the Eiffel Tower. In the Luxembourg Gardens. In front of luxury hotels and mansions. Sometimes even in parking lots. \n  \n A rage, a trend, a new tradition feeding a lucrative branch of the tourism industry and spreading not only around the most romantic city of the world, but to many other places in France, Great Britain, Greece, Italy and other European countries. \n  \n Traveling from all over Asia, including Hong Kong, Singapore, Japan, Indonesia and Malaysia, brides and grooms arrive by the hundreds every month to pose in their wedding outfits for romantic photos alongside some of the most famous landmarks - and lately next to less well-known sites. \n  \n In Paris, they're becoming as ubiquitous as street vendors. \n  \n Although they look like newlyweds, clad in white wedding dresses, veils, tuxedos, flowers and all the accoutrements, they are, in fact, \"pre-wedding\" events organized just for the photos. The real wedding takes place back home, sometimes months later, where showing the portraits as slide shows, posters and videos on big screens is an important part of the celebration. \n  \n “Most couples take the wedding photos months before the wedding, and it can be a complicated and arduous process that demands days of effort, numerous costume changes, exotic locations and a huge budget, with some shoots costing as much as $15,000,\" according to research on the pre-wedding industry by Li Zhaoping at China’s Saimaa University. \"As opposed to Western couples who seek wedding pictures that capture real moments, Asian couples want elaborate photos that reflect their romantic fantasies.” \n  \n It's not a new fad. In reality, affluent Asian couples have been doing it for years. What's new is that thanks to cheaper travel, their numbers have been steadily growing, creating a prosperous industry that now offers one or multiple photographers, many choices of romantic sites throughout Europe and more than just stylish photos in wedding finery. \n  \n Florists, hair dressers, makeup artists, brand party gowns, shoes, limos and vintage cars are part of the packages destined to fulfill couples' most romantic dreams - at least in pictures. \n  \n “A love dream that has a price of about €2,000 to travel to Paris from miles away to take dozens of photos in one day and bring them back home,” explains France 2. \n  \n An article in Le Monde about the particular tourist wave reports that there are several “wedding agencies” in Paris where the couples can rent either red dresses, which is traditional Chinese, or bride white dresses “a la Parisienne,” shoes, tuxedos, jewels, lace and veils, and arrange hairdressing and makeup. \n  \n “In the basement, the brides can try hairstyles and have makeup tests,\" one such agency manager told the paper. \"On the day of the photos, they will be manicured and adorned with false eyelashes.” \n  \n Many choose to change multiple times during shoots and appear in glamorous gowns and chic outfits to obtain dreamy images against romantic backdrops like England's White Cliffs of Dover, lavender fields in the south of France, the island of Santorini in Greece and Big Ben in London. \n  \n A beautiful, 13th-century Cotswold parish church, for example, got caught up in the fad after a couple took beautiful quintessentially English photos there. Bath and the Lake District are also very popular. \n  \n Some of the expanding services offer elaborate extremes in sessions that can last several days, with lavish photos in flimsy frocks on location such as famous ski resorts for photos in the snow, or at places where popular movies or TV series were filmed. \n  \n In a South China Morning Post article about the popular tradition it calls “The Downton Abbey effect on Chinese pre-wedding photo shoots,” the paper finds that “fueled by the popular period drama, hundreds of middle-class couples fly to Britain every year and spend thousands of pounds to hire teams of photographers for shoots in locations such as Oxford, Cambridge, Brighton, York and Cornwall’s Land’s End.” \n  \n The trend, according to the paper, “has been fueled in part by the popularity of Downton Abbey, which drew 160 million viewers in China, and by a Chinese superstar singer-actor, Jay Chou, who married in Yorkshire's Selby Abbey, then held a reception in Castle Howard, made famous by the 1980s TV adaptation of Evelyn Waugh’s Brideshead Revisited.\" \n  \n A £5,000 fee at Castle Howard, which is one of the high-end packages, includes entry and a day of shots inside and outside. A carriage and horse costs another £550. \n  \n An average package starts at €1,000 for basic nuptial photos at a landmark. Adding two other locations can cost an additional €500 to €1,000, depending of the distance. \n  \n “On a sweltering hot July afternoon,” describes the South China Morning Post, “they spend four hours earnestly kissing, holding hands, striking film-star poses and running through lavender in what looks like an exhausting session. For one series of shots, the groom carries his betrothed in his arms for 30 paces.” \n  \n Many other places in France, Britain and other countries are aspiring to achieve a spot on the map of pre-wedding photos choices. \n  \n According to Le Monde, the booming Asian pre-wedding industry in France - “homeland of romanticism, offering its most beautiful shots to couples from the Far East, among pigeons flying in front of Notre-Dame, dancing in a French garden, at a Bateau-Mouche sailing in front of the illuminated Eiffel tower, at a castle of the XV century or among fields of lavender” - is estimated to be worth more than €1 billion per year. ||