d providing extra insulation. Their rounded wings allow them to take off quickly in case of danger and enable agile flight even at high altitudes.\n\nThe Ptarmigan's diet mainly consists of plant matter, such as seeds, leaves, berries, and buds. In winter, when vegetation is scarce, they rely on dwarf willow and birch, digging under the snow to find food. Ptarmigans have a sturdy and short beak, which is adapted to peck at hard materials, allowing them to break through ice and frozen plants in search of sustenance.\n\nThe Ptarmigan is an exceptional example of a bird that has evolved a variety of unique adaptations to survive in some of the most extreme environments on Earth. Its winter-white camouflage, insulating plumage, feathered feet, rounded wings, and specialized diet all contribute to its ability to thrive in the harsh mountainous and arctic habitats of Britain."}]}
