n a material, a vector wouldn't suffice; a tensor would be required here because the change in material is happening along multiple axes and needs a multi-directional representation, which tensors are adept at capturing.\n\nThe dimensionality of tensors is indeed key, as tensors allow for the representation and manipulation of complex high-dimensional maps in way vectors cannot. Apart from representing multi-dimensional physical entities, they are also used in fields like machine learning and data science for high-dimensional data manipulation.\n\nTherefore, these two questions are not paraphrases of one another. The first question asks for the difference between vector and tensor, while the second one seeks to understand the importance or necessity for tensors in the context of vectors."}]}
