.S.C. 1825(b)) and either the \n                                period for seeking judicial review of \n                                the final agency action has lapsed or \n                                there has been a final judgment with \n                                respect to an appeal of such \n                                assessment, or\n                                    ``(II) has been convicted under \n                                section 19(d) of the Animal Welfare Act \n                                (7 U.S.C. 2149(d)) or section 6(a) of \n                                the Horse Protection Act (15 U.S.C. \n                                1825(a)) and there is a final judgment \n                                with respect to such conviction.\n                            ``(ii) Aggregation rules.--All persons \n                        treated as a single employer under subsection \n                        (a) or (b) of section 52, or subsection (m) or \n                        (o) of section 414, shall be treated as one \n                        taxpayer for purposes of this subparagraph.\n                    ``(B) Applicable period.--The term `applicable \n                period' means, with respect to any violation described \n                in subparagraph (A), the 5-taxable-year period \n                beginning with the taxable year in which the period for \n                seeking judicial review of a civil penalty described in \n                subparagraph (A)(i) has lapsed or in which there has \n                been a final judgment entered with respect to the \n                violation, whichever is earlier.''.\n    (c) Conforming Amendment.--The last sentence of section 179(d)(1) \nis amended by inserting ``or any property placed in service by a \ndisqualified taxpayer (as defined in section 168(g)(8)(A)) during an \napplicable period (as defined in section 168(g)(8)(B))'' after \n``section 50(b)''.\n    (d) Effective Date.--The amendments made by this section shall \napply to property placed in service in taxable years beginning after \nthe date of the enactment of this section.\nA:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The Animal Welfare Accountability and Transparency Act aims to make information about animal welfare in the United States more publicly accessible and amend tax implications for those who violate animal protection rules. The main provisions of this bill include:\n\n1. Public availability of regulatory records: Within 90 days of the enactment of the bill, the Secretary of Agriculture is to maintain and make available an online searchable database of information relating to the administration of the Animal Welfare Act and the Horse Protection Act. This includes inspection reports, enforcement actions, research facility annual reports, and the contact information for licensed facilities, exhibitors, dealers, and persons involved in horse promotion.\n\n2. Use of alternative depreciation system for taxpayers violating certain animal protection rules: The bill amends the Internal Revenue Code, making taxpayers who violate animal protection rules (and have been assessed penalties or convicted under the mentioned acts) use an alternative depreciation system. This would apply to property placed in service during a five-year period following the lapse of time for seeking judicial review or the entry of a final judgment in the case.\n\nThese provisions aim to increase transparency in animal protection and impose tax consequences for those who violate animal welfare regulations."}]}
