{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. You will be given a task. You must generate a detailed and long answer."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Please write a short summary for the following article:\n\nThe Real Mess Behind Mariagate\n\nForgive me for asking, but I was out of the country last week and on the road a day or two back here at home. Do I have this right– Citigroup Chairman Charles Prince Charles Prince , riding hard astride the financial colossus with $140 billion in annual revenue and $1.5 trillion in assets, has just fired a high-roller lieutenant for allegedly having an illicit affair with the Money Honey from CNBC?\n\nOf course it doesn’t read quite that way in some financial media, which devoted abundant space to the flap–that would be way too specific and far too difficult to prove–even as they feasted on it on their front pages. But that is precisely what they and the rest of the media are hinting at, in weasly words of innuendo and wimpy off-the-record insinuation. This gossipfest for a pimped-out press likely comes from Citigroup sympathizers–maybe even from in-house leakers.\n\nAnd so publications devote precious column inches in tacit pursuit of trivial trysting that may never have happened–and get diverted from the real story, the far bigger one that actually matters: Citigroup is faltering, Chuck Prince’s power and his reign are at risk, and he has just fired a hitter who was loyal to the man Prince succeeded: the behemoth’s patriarch and architect, Sanford I. Weill. Come to think of it, this sounds like a shrewd crisis-PR strategy. Shame on all of you.\n\nIn case you missed it: Last week Todd Thomson Todd Thomson , chief of Citigroup’s wealth management unit, got whacked by Prince, officially for excessive spending and such, but unofficially for what one financial newspaper genteely described as “the way that Mr. Thomson managed his friendship with” CNBC anchor Maria Bartiromo. “Managed his friendship”? Cue video of the old Monty Python sketch with Eric Idle elbowing the fellow next to him and asking about sex: “Nudge nudge, snap snap, grin grin, wink wink, say no more?”\n\nNever mind that the stock of this financial services laggard has gone up only 12% in three years (while the S&P 500 is up 28%), that Citi ranks a lowly 28th of 42 banks in our own Platinum List performance rankings, and that it routinely trails Bank of America in return on assets. And never mind whether Citigroup, to unlock better value, should undergo a smashup and break itself up entirely or peddle some businesses to private equity buyers. (Sell the credit-card biz! Bail out of consumer banking!) Instead, let’s get the media machine going ’round and ’round over whether Thomson might have scored on the corporate jet.\n\nBut Citigroup shareholders dont care whether a Citigroup executive might have cheated on his wife, and Citigroup leakers shouldn’t, either. None of this has any bearing on business: It just doesnt matter. What Citigroup shareholders do care about is the stock price and what Prince is doing to lift it–and how he manages the company. And there’s plenty here to warrant concern.\n\nWe are told Prince sacked his second as a cost-crackdown move because Thomson had a palatial fireplace installed in his office, but one wonders where the chairman was when the jackhammers were grinding away at the start. We are told Prince had warned Thomson to cut back on Maria-time at client functions–yet she adds star power that can make Citi look good in exchange for her access to the bigs in the room.\n\nWe are told Prince was “jolted” (in the lead of The Wall Street Journal‘s Page One exposé on Jan. 24) by Thomson’s temerity in putting $5 million into a short-run TV series on the Sundance Channel to be hosted by Robert Redford and–omidgod!–the dreaded Bartiromo. Yet five million bucks in a $140 billion company? That is 1.3% of the revenue that Citi brings in–in a single, 24-hour day. Moreover, its a cheap way to align Citi with the greenie movement and host super-rich private banking clients on the film festival circuit.\n\nAnd we are told, as the Journal intoned, with an upraised eyebrow, that Maria Bartiromo and Todd Thomson were “spotted” at the plush eatery Daniel in New York in late 2005, dining alone. “Word of the sighting spread through Citigroup the next day.” Sure it did–the foreign currency traders must have gone wild. Let’s get this right: A journalist working her sources goes out to dinner with a source who runs a big business for a huge company she covers. Isn’t that what journos do all the time?\n\nSomething odious and sexist and archaic drives these artfully veiled accounts. Bartiromo, who plays it straight and is fiercely committed to her job, would not have been made a target if she weighed 200 pounds and looked like Winston Churchill. And Thomson wouldn’t have gotten pilloried in the press if there weren’t enemies inside or close to Citi feeding off-the-record invective to the writers.\n\nLet us get back to business: Citi has 1,000 banking branches in the U.S., while Bank of America has six times as many; it has failed to reap billions in private equity bets the way Goldman Sachs routinely does; and it runs third in credit cards. If the numbers suck when the company reports earnings in coming weeks and quarters, we’ll know the real reason for this brouhaha: Mariagate was a way to distract attention from the real screw-ups at Citigroup itself.\n\nComments are turned off for this post.\n\nSummary:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The article discusses the dismissal of Todd Thomson, chief of Citigroup's wealth management unit by Citigroup Chairman Charles Prince. The dismissal is unofficially linked to Thomson's relationship with CNBC anchor Maria Bartiromo, though officially claimed to be for excessive spending. The author argues that the media focus on this potential scandal diverts attention from the real issue: Citigroup's poor performance, with a stock increase of only 12% in three years and a low ranking on the Platinum List performance rankings. The author suggests that the media should focus on Citigroup's management and business performance over alleged personal scandals."}]}
