e dissenters were from England and followed the Christian faith.\n- \"Separated from the Church of England\": This phrase also emphasizes the act of separation, as in the first statement.\n- \"16th, 17th, and 18th centuries\": The time frame is identical, indicating the same period of dissent and separation from the Church.\n\nIn summary, both statements mean the same, describing English Christians who separated from the Church of England (also known as the 'English Church') in the 16th, 17th, and 18th centuries and labeling them as either Dissidents, Dissenters or Protestant Separatists. The minor difference between the phrasing used ('Dissident' vs 'English Dissenter') does not change the overall meaning of the statements."}]}
