ving 30 sheep and a quantity of barley. Thereafter, the islands developed a reputation for abundance. At the time of Martin's visit in 1697 the population was 180 and the steward travelled with a \"company\" of up to 60 persons to which he \"elected the most 'meagre' among his friends in the neighbouring islands, to that number and took them periodically to St. Kilda to enjoy the nourishing and plentiful, if primitive, fare of the island, and so be restored to their wonted health and strength.\"\nA:"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The present-day name of the place John Fordun referred to as 'the isle of Irte (insula de Irte), which is agreed to be under the Circius and on the margins of the world' is St Kilda. This can be inferred from the article's mention of St Kilda's first written record dating from 1202, and its discussion of the islands being historically part of the domain of the MacLeods of Harris. The association of St Kilda with the name 'isle of Irte' mentioned by John of Fordun further supports this conclusion."}]}
