{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are an AI assistant. You will be given a task. You must generate a detailed and long answer."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Write a title for this article:\n\nAged just 28, Sheryl Sandberg was already chief of staff to the then US treasury secretary Larry Summers. She moved to Google in 2001, where she was closely involved in the launch of Google’s philanthropic arm, Google.org.\n\nSandberg was recruited as chief operating officer of Facebook after meeting founder Mark Zuckerberg at a Christmas party, and is largely credited with turning the social media platform into the highly profitable company it is today.\n\nSandberg was the first woman on Facebook’s board of directors and has been active as a board member of several non-profit organisations, and has built the Lean In Foundation, a charity and online community for women.\n\nHowever it was her book Lean In, published in 2013, which caught the imagination of women around the world. Sandberg argues that, “Women should be encouraged to ‘sit at the table’”, seek challenges, take risks and pursue their goals with gusto, urging women “to set boundaries and abandon the myth of ‘having it all’.”\n\nArianna Stassinopoulos Huffington is a Greek-born American businesswoman and media entrepreneur who co-founded news website The Huffington Post.\n\nUnder her stewardship, The Huffington Post has become one of the most influential and important news and comment websites in the US, the first purely digital publisher to be awarded a Pulitzer Prize.\n\nShe left The Huffington Post in August this year to begin a new start-up, Thrive Global, promoting health and wellness in the workplace.\n\nHuffington says that the world as it is creates stress and burnout for both sexes, but especially for women, who tend not to thrive in male-dominated working environments. “We need to redesign it,” she says. “And women have a great opportunity to do that.”\n\nAged just 23, Karren Brady earned her nickname “The First Lady of British Football”, when she was made the first female managing director of a British football club, Birmingham City. And she became the youngest managing director of a PLC when the company was floated in 1997.\n\nNow vice-chairman of West Ham, Brady stars in BBC’s The Apprentice alongside host Lord Sugar and was the small business Ambassador for the UK during David Cameron’s government, as well as being a strong advocate and champion for women in business.\n\nIn 2014 Brady was awarded a life peerage as Baroness Brady of Knightsbridge, and was in addition made a Commander of the British Empire for services to entrepreneurship and women in business.\n\nSays Brady, “Those women who have got to the top need to ensure there is a pipeline of younger women, whether by networking or mentoring, who in turn are encouraging those below them.”\n\nDuring her long and illustrious career with Nickelodeon, Fox and Disney, Anne Sweeney was often known as the most powerful woman in entertainment. After overseeing the global expansion of Nickelodeon in the late 1980s, Sweeney moved to FX networks, where she launched FXM: Movies from Fox, Hollywood’s first studio-based movie network.\n\nIn 1996 Sweeney moved to The Walt Disney Company as president of the Disney Channel and became president of ABC Networks, responsible for Disney’s entertainment and news television properties worldwide. Hugely successful and respected wherever she has been, Sweeney recently surprised many in the industry by quitting her executive career for a new role as a director.\n\nSweeney is a supporter of Girls Inc., a charity which “inspires all girls to be strong, smart and bold”, empowering girls to be healthy, educated and to succeed.\n\nSweeney said: “Define success on your own terms, achieve it by your own rules, and build a life you’re proud to live.”\n\nWhoopi Goldberg once said: “We’re all here for a reason. I believe a bit of the reason is to throw little torches out to lead